WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.609 --> 00:00:08.340
Hey what's up guys?! It's Rob. I hope
you're all doing well. I've been learning

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.849
how to do some different things lately
learning some animation though I'm not

00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:15.240
really good at it, but maybe if I
practice enough I'll get better since

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.539
it's always something I wanted to try. It
was also my cat Leela's 12th birthday

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:22.769
last week on April 19th and I posted
some pictures and videos on my Instagram

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:26.400
IG: TheRobWorld so be sure to check that
out I'll post a link down below. I've

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.550
always wanted to do a juggling tutorial
'cuz I think it's something that everyone

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.820
should really know how to do. It also
seems about 80% of people on my

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.180
community poll want to see it also. The
definition of juggling that I've always

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.230
remembered is the manipulation of more
objects than you have hands: so two or

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:44.700
more objects in one hand or three or
more objects in two hands. Juggling is

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.370
not only fun and entertaining but it's
extremely good for your hand-eye

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:51.660
coordination and reflexes and also your
left and right brain balance. For this

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.840
tutorial I'm going to teach you how to
do the Cascade which basically involves

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.500
the balls going in a figure eight motion
there is a way to do it that they go in

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.480
a circle but I'll leave that for another
time.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.629
Juggling is something that you do need
to practice to get good at. It might take

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:07.680
you a couple hours, days, it depends on
you and how much you put into it.  Optimally,

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.520
we want to start with some bean bag balls
just like these I do offer these on my

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.090
Amazon Store that you can find in the
links down below.  Using really bouncy

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:18.299
balls like hand balls and tennis balls
aren't really the best to start with but

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:21.630
if you really have nothing and you want
to try it out you can also take a wet or

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:26.060
dry paper towel and wrap it in aluminum
foil like this. So guys.. What do you say?

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:33.630
Want to learn how to juggle? Let's do it!
I learned how to juggle for my brother

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:37.439
Rick over 15 years ago and when I got to
college someone tossed me two Ketchup

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:40.439
packets at the same time and when I
caught them they said "How'd you do that?"

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:48.080
and I said "cuz I juggle" #CuzIJuggle

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.840
Making sure our feet are shoulder-width
apart and our hands a little bit above

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.860
our waist with our palms up, we're going
to imagine a window frame in front of us

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.420
Our eyes are looking at the top of that
frame a little bit above our head, right

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.620
around where the ball will hit its Apex
or Peak. When we throw, we're going to

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.730
keep the balls within that plane and its
boundaries and our eyes at the top of

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:52.260
the frame we need to therefore develop
the ability to catch the ball by sort of

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:56.880
predicting where it will land and not
looking directly at it. I'm left-handed

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.329
and start with the ball in my left hand
and throw it to my right but you might

00:03:00.329 --> 00:03:03.360
prefer starting with your right hand.
We're trying to toss it between our

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.950
hands in an arc as precisely and
consistently as possible. Remember we

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:11.160
need to say within the plane and
dimensions of the window frame so we

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:15.150
don't throw the ball too far back or
forward or move our feet. We also don't

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:19.260
want to throw the ball too high or
straight up. Once you've practiced enough

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.500
with one ball and feel that you can
consistently and accurately throw it

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.010
back and forth between your hands in an
arc, then you're ready to give two balls

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:30.630
a shot. With one ball in each hand now
we're going to stand with our hands

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:34.079
shoulder-width apart and waist-high
again with our eyes glancing slightly

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:38.100
above our head. With our starting hand
we'll first toss the ball to our

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.299
opposite hand,  and when that first ball
reaches the apex we can then throw the

00:03:42.299 --> 00:03:47.400
second ball from our opposite hand up
and under the first one in an arc to our

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:51.959
starting hand. After the second ball is
thrown from our opposite hand we then

00:03:51.959 --> 00:03:55.409
need to use that same hand to catch the
first ball that we threw and that's

00:03:55.409 --> 00:03:58.880
landing. Then we can get ready to catch
the second ball

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:04.130
our starting hand. As you can see here, I
start with the two balls one in each

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:10.280
hand and first throw with my left hand
and exchange the balls. I then start with

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.210
my right hand and do the same exact
thing the opposite way. It can be really

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:21.920
helpful to call the balls out as you
throw "One Two."

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.190
Once you get good at it you should

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:30.860
try to practice continuously without
stopping like this. You're not trying to

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:35.200
pass the balls between your hands like
this as a lot of beginners tend to do

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.080
You need to get the timing and rhythm
down so that you're not throwing the

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.220
ball too early and possibly hitting them
together or even throwing it too late

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:48.230
and causing you to rush and mess up. As
always, make sure that you're staying

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:52.190
within the plane of the window frame and
that you're making accurate consistent

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:56.180
throw is side to side. Otherwise you'll
find yourself making unnecessary

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:59.750
movements which can affect you when you
try to juggle three. Once you feel

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.870
confident that you've gotten the hang of
throwing two balls, there's a few

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:06.740
training exercises I'd like to do before
I attempt three balls. While these are

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:10.130
optional, they can further help you
improve your reflexes and hand-eye

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:14.450
coordination and will help you learn 3 a
lot easier. One of the more challenging

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:18.410
exercises is to take two balls in your
starting hand and throw them one by one

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:22.730
across to your opposite hand. Once you
get good at it, the goal is to throw the

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:27.020
first ball across and then the second
one, only to quickly throw the first ball

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:32.210
back to your starting hand before the
second ball lands. Then throw the second

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:36.140
ball after it creating what looks like a
figure eight or snake if done

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:43.490
continuously. Another good exercise is to
learn to juggle two balls in one hand.

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:47.210
To do this you toss the first ball up and
when it hits the peak, throw the second

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:51.740
one after. Then again try to do it
continuously, almost in a circular or

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:55.760
elliptical pattern. While it's also
difficult, if you can master this

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:00.800
juggling three balls in two hands will
become much easier. Also, juggling two

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:04.490
balls in each of your hands
simultaneously is the basis for juggling

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:08.660
four balls, as you saw at the beginning.
To juggle three balls we now need to add

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:12.529
one more ball into the mix and we do
this by placing two balls

00:06:12.529 --> 00:06:16.459
our starting hand and one ball in the
opposite hand it doesn't matter which

00:06:16.459 --> 00:06:19.519
hand you start with, it's usually
whichever you feel comfortable with, and

00:06:19.519 --> 00:06:24.319
I use my left hand. We throw the first
ball from our starting hand and as you

00:06:24.319 --> 00:06:28.699
could see here it's represented by the
red line. Just as that ball hits the peak

00:06:28.699 --> 00:06:33.109
or apex of its path we then get ready to
throw the next ball, or the one

00:06:33.109 --> 00:06:37.609
represented by the blue line, in our
opposite hand. As we throw the second

00:06:37.609 --> 00:06:41.779
ball (in blue here) up and under the first
we then have to get ready to catch the

00:06:41.779 --> 00:06:46.489
one that's landing (here in red) you can
think of this as an exchange or switch

00:06:46.489 --> 00:06:51.379
in that we get rid of the ball that's in
our hand, and after it has gone out, we

00:06:51.379 --> 00:06:55.429
then catch the one that's falling or
coming in. Once the second ball (here in

00:06:55.429 --> 00:06:59.959
blue) hits its apex we then get ready to
throw the third ball here in green up

00:06:59.959 --> 00:07:04.189
and under it just as we've been doing
before. Once again, now that we've thrown

00:07:04.189 --> 00:07:07.789
the last ball we have to get ready to
catch the ball that's falling down or

00:07:07.789 --> 00:07:11.989
the second ball in blue. This is why
accurate throws are important. Just as

00:07:11.989 --> 00:07:15.409
we're lifting up our hand, the blue ball
should kind of be landing right in it.

00:07:15.409 --> 00:07:19.669
Now, this is where the cycle repeats
itself. As the last ball is in the air

00:07:19.669 --> 00:07:24.110
and Peaks, we now have to begin to throw
the first ball, which is now in our

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:28.399
opposite hand up and under it just as
we've been doing. Demonstrated here I

00:07:28.399 --> 00:07:32.479
first place two balls in my starting
hand and one in my opposite hand and I

00:07:32.479 --> 00:07:36.229
make the first throw from my starting
hand. I'm going to first try to make

00:07:36.229 --> 00:07:41.239
three throws and three catches. Once I've
done that, then I practice with my

00:07:41.239 --> 00:07:46.309
opposite hand starting first. When you
feel comfortable and the last ball is in

00:07:46.309 --> 00:07:49.959
the air, try and continue with another
for a fourth throw and catch.

00:07:49.959 --> 00:07:55.909
Then try five. Stay focused and don't
panic, and try to keep going and once

00:07:55.909 --> 00:08:01.149
you've done about twenty catches or so
you can really say you're juggling

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.620
Remember, juggling is all about making
accurate and consistent throws and

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:12.160
staying focused once you see the ball in
its peak you need to almost

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:16.060
instinctively know where it's going to
land without looking you'll find when

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:19.420
you practice your hands will almost
start to move on their own to catch them

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:23.260
Once you've learned how to juggle, it's
something you'll never really forget and

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:25.300
you'll definitely want to try with other
objects

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:29.170
(hopefully things that won't break). Thank
you guys so much for watching my

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:32.620
tutorial on How to Juggle. I worked really
hard and I had a lot of fun making it so

